# Swift & macOS

## Concurrency
- UI, `CollegePersistence`, and repositories are `@MainActor` — don't call them from nonisolated/sync contexts without `MainActor.run` or isolation fixes.
- `Sendable` closures must not capture actor-isolated state unsafely; extract `nonisolated static` helpers when bridging actors (e.g. scraper page callbacks).

## Data layer
- New feature code should use repositories (`CareerRepository`, etc.) directly; `CollegePersistence.shared` is a legacy façade.
- Persist through existing merge/coalesce paths (`ModelMergeCoalescer`, `bumpCareerRevision`) — don't invent parallel save logic.

## AppKit + SwiftUI
- SwiftUI owns `NSSplitView` via `NSSplitViewController`. **Never** call `NSSplitView.setHoldingPriority` on controller-managed splits — set `splitViewItems[].holdingPriority` instead.
- Don't assign custom `NSSplitView` delegates when SwiftUI owns the view; use position pinning + drift correction patterns in `MainSidebarSplitAutosave`.
- Introspect AppKit (`NSViewRepresentable`) only when SwiftUI lacks the API; keep side effects idempotent and keyed per view instance.

## File style
- Keep the module header block (`// File.swift`, `// Feature:`, `// Purpose:`) when editing existing files.
- Prefer `guard` + early return over deep nesting; use typed errors already defined in the module.

```swift
// ❌ Crashes under NavigationSplitView
splitView.setHoldingPriority(.init(1000), forSubviewAt: 0)

// ✅ Controller-owned split
controller.splitViewItems[0].holdingPriority = .init(1000)
```
